                <head>202. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At the 392d meeting of the National Security Council, December 23, Allen Dulles touched upon Laos in his briefing,
                    “Significant World Developments Affecting U.S. Security.” His remarks read as
                    follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Mr. Dulles noted that in Laos
                            drastic changes in the government will be required if the Pathet Lao
                            element is to be restrained. A showdown with Communist elements might be
                            in the offing in this country. The President inquired about Communism in
                            the Laotian Army. Mr. Dulles
                            thought the top leadership of the Army was anti-Communist.” (Memorandum
                            of discussion by Boggs; Eisenhower Library, Whitman File,
                                NSC Records)</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
